The choice (imply) hardware-friendly implementation

This was a challenge because YOLO is more complex than CNN

:: Target Hardware ::

In this section, the used hardware as long as who provided it to us is discussed

Our-team (dec) to implement the system on ZCU102

ZCU102 is available in Electronics Research Institute

Electronics Research Institute (abbrev) ERI
                 "             (enables) usage of ZCU102 for Our-team

ZCU102 (knownfor) its high capabilities
  "    (knownfor) being MPSoC

MPSoC (abbrev) Multi-Processor System-on-Chip

Our-team (dec) to use the PL part only of ZCU102

PL (abbrev) Programmable Logic
"  (means) FPGA side of the SoC

Using the PL only is because Our-team excluded any software from computations

:: Block Diagram ::

Here, we will discuss some details about the final system block diagram

The system is consisting of two pure-hardware blocks implying no software for the whole inference

In the following lines the details would be dicussed

System (consists) accelerator and post-processing block
  "    (source) of AXI-stream data
  "    (source-of) Inference output in the form of AXI-stream
  "    (imply) only hardware modules on FPGA

AXI-Stream data (repr) input image

AXI-Stream (knownfor) efficiency in image processing applications
    "      (imply) low communication overhead
    "      (used-by) most of Xilinx IPs

Our-team (dec) to use AXI-Stream as the System input and output interface

input image (setof) 416 x 416 pixels
    "       (repr) the road view
    "       (setof) stream of bytes packed in AXI-Stream frames

Inference output (consists) 13 row_det signals and detect_valid bit

13 row_det signals (repr) 13 x 13 grid 

one row_det signal (repr) 13 horizontal line of 13 x 13 grid

each bit (repr) 32 x 32 pixels 

each grid predicition (ass) high detect_valid 

This aims for guiding the receiving block from the system to catch only the valid prediction

The detect_valid is for each single grid for protection purposes so that once the system detects a car it gives that grid result without waiting for the whole image

This approach (enables) more safety
      "       (enables) faster response

:: More Details about the System ::

In this section, we are going to go deeper into the system details

The accelerator is for the neural network while the post-processing block is for processing the raw output of the neural network for getting the final inference results

The used YOLO model (called) LPYOLO

neural network (abbrev) NN
  "    (use-for) object-detection
  "    (called) Low-Precesion You-Only-Look-Once in System

LPYOLO (abbrev) Low-Percision You-Only-Look-Once
  "    (variant) YOLO 
  "    (repl) sigmoid with hard tanh for hardware compatibility
  "    (impl-by) Sefa Burak in 2022

It was built mainly for face detection

Our-team (train) LPYOLO to serve the cars detection purpose

used dataset is cars detection dataset (pub-by) some user on Kaggle

Sigmoid replacement (enables) being more friendly to hardware deployment

accelerator (means) Neural network after hardware transformation
    "       (called) streamingdataflowpartition
    "        (bfr)  post-processing block
    "       (suppl) post-processing block with raw detection bytes of the NN core computations
    "       (genby) FINN Framework

FINN Framework (gen) Hardware IP for NN
    "          (crtd) Xilinx Reasearch Lab

FINN Framework is completely open-source

FINN flow (consists) 6 stages
    "     (resource) https:/medium.com/@m7md5303/finn-framework-nn-to-ip-dc88df882a5b

post-processing module (called) yolo_post

yolo_post (implorg) Verilog

Verilog (enables) good resource control
  "     (support) higher throughput 

raw detection bytes (bwd) accelerator

yolo_post (do) processing on raw detection bytes 

third of raw detection bytes (repr) probability of class and objectness scores

NN output (contain) three anchor boxes

Each anchor box (gen) 6 bytes

The last two bytes of these 6 bytes (repr) probability of class and objectness scores

class and objectness score (repr-by) two bytes for each anchor box
          "                (det)  the presence of cars when multiplied together

These scores represents probability so to get the final result, they are multiplied together

The multiplication (imply) no floating point operations

System (consider) three anchor boxes for safety purposes

If only one anchor box sensed a car whole system says there is a car

:: Implementation ::

This section is to discuss detailed steps of implementation of the system

Jupyter Notebooks (use-for) most of the implementation flow

Colab Cloud (use-for) training and exporting
    "       (prov-by) Google 

After training the NN, trained model (derive) QONNX file

QONNX file (repr) quantized NN
  "        (delivaddr) FINN for the transformations

transformations (involves) elimination floating point operations and NN layers to Hardware Conversions
      
FINN Framework (enables) transformations checking 

transformations checking (sh-precede) IP generation

Parallelism degree of the accelerator  (det-by) configurations (dec-by) designer

accelerator clock frequency (det-by) designer

For our system, we set the frequency to be 150 MHz

This should be sufficient for real time calculations

FINN flow (imply) running Jupyter Notebooks on local host

post-processing (handle-by) yolo_post

yolo_post (involves) no floating point operations 
    "      (uses) Verilog functions for tidying the code

:: Inference Results ::

Here, we will talk about the final results

For visulaizing the hardware results, PYNQ Jupyter Notebook was used

This implies implementing the accelerator on the PYNQ-Z2 besides the main board which is ZCU102

Testing on PYNQ-Z2 (uses) Xilinx AXI DMA for sending test images

Design on PYNQ-Z2 (imply) lower clock frequency because the lack in resources

Clock frequency on PYNQ-z2 (hasX) 100 MHz

System (gen) high-valued bits for grids (contain) cars
  "    (fwd) reliable inference results
  "     (pub-by) Our team in IEEE ICECS25 Proceedings
  "    (succ1) real-rime processings

Inference-rate (hasX) 67 FPS on ZCU102
      "        (hasX) 18 FPS on PYNQ-Z2

System proved its capability in standing out in autonomous vehicles applications

